        <!-- ===================== EXPLANATION BOX ===================== -->
        <mxCell id="explain" parent="1" vertex="1"
          value="&lt;b&gt;Why diagonal banking?&lt;/b&gt;&#10;&#10;A vector register holds a 2D image grid (8&#215;4 shown; 128&#215;128 in hardware). Its pixels are spread across 8 SRAM banks by the rule &lt;b&gt;bank = (row + col) mod 8&lt;/b&gt;.&#10;&#10;Because every pixel on one anti-diagonal (constant row+col) maps to the same bank, any full &lt;i&gt;row&lt;/i&gt;, any full &lt;i&gt;column&lt;/i&gt;, or any &lt;i&gt;diagonal&lt;/i&gt; of the grid touches each of the 8 banks at most once. The hardware can therefore read all those access patterns in parallel with &lt;b&gt;no bank conflicts&lt;/b&gt; &#8212; the key to transposing / sliding the image cheaply."
          style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FAFAFA;strokeColor=#9E9E9E;strokeWidth=1;dashed=1;fontSize=13;fontColor=#212121;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=12;spacingTop=10;spacingRight=12;">
          <mxGeometry x="160" y="640" width="700" height="170" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
